# Geometry optimization of water with an LDA reference: `--optimize --wf=scf`.
#
# The three LiH optimization cases all share a blind spot that this one covers,
# and each part of it was a real defect:
#
#   * LiH is a LINEAR DIATOMIC -- one internal degree of freedom, and the
#     rotation about the molecular axis is null, so the translation/rotation
#     projector is barely exercised. Water is bent: 3 coupled internal degrees
#     of freedom and a soft bend, which is where a mis-scaled convergence
#     threshold actually shows up.
#
#   * they all run `xc hf`. This is the only optimization case on a functional.
#
#   * they all pin `eprec 1e-6`. This deck deliberately leaves `eprec` at its
#     1e-4 default, because that is what a user typing
#     `madqc --geometry=h2o --optimize --dft="xc=lda"` gets.
#
# The raw derivatives here carry a spurious net force of ~1.1e-3 a.u. along z
# that does NOT shrink with eprec; MolOpt projects it out and prints what is
# left (`MolOpt::print_gradient`). Before the thresholds were tied to `dconv`,
# `gtol` was derived at 1e-5 -- below the gradient's own noise floor -- and this
# calculation took 7 geometry steps of non-monotonic wandering instead of 4,
# with MolOpt's "insufficient precision" escape hatch armed too tightly to fire.
#
# Run:   ./run.sh    (or: madqc --optimize --wf=scf scf_h2o_lda_optimize.in)

optimization
    maxiter     10
end

dft
    xc          lda
    protocol    [1e-4, 1e-6]
    dconv       1e-4
end

molecule
    units   atomic
    O   0.0   0.0          -0.21300717
    H   0.0   1.4265081     0.85202867
    H   0.0  -1.4265081     0.85202867
end
